# {{.Name}}

A [nautilus](https://github.com/joyautomation/nautilus) controller — IEC
61131-3 logic, run and shipped by the `nautilus` CLI. No Go in this
project: the logic is in the IEC files, the wiring is `nautilus.yaml`, and
nothing here needs a toolchain.

```sh
nautilus run        # scan loop + dashboard + tag API on http://localhost:8080
nautilus test       # acceptance tests, in virtual time
nautilus check      # compile (CI gate)
nautilus build      # emit ./{{.Name}} — a self-contained controller binary
```

{{if .Demo -}}
## What's here

| File | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `nautilus.yaml` | the wiring: three tasks, tags by role, server, driver |
| `program.fbd` | control at 10 Hz — pump seal-in latch, heater PI, `TON` alarm delay |
| `sim.st` | the plant, simulated in Structured Text (delete it for real I/O) |
| `interlocks.ld` | the annunciator in ladder, on its own 200 ms task |
| `blocks.st` | a user `FUNCTION_BLOCK`, callable from any language |
| `{{.Name}}_test.yaml` | acceptance tests, including the 10 s alarm delay |

Three tasks, three IEC languages, one tag store — the resource/task model
from the standard. Right-click `program.fbd` → *Open With → FBD Diagram*,
or `interlocks.ld` → *Ladder Diagram*: live values paint power flow while
the controller runs, and every gesture is a structural edit to the text.

## Going to real I/O

Delete the `sim` task from `nautilus.yaml` and change `LevelPct`/`TempC`
to `role: input`. The control program doesn't change — it reads the same
tags either way. Then point `driver:` at the field:

```yaml
driver:
  type: eip
  host: 192.168.1.10
  manifest: eip_manifest.yaml   # from `nautilus eip import`
```

{{else -}}
The logic is `program.{{.Language}}`; `nautilus.yaml` declares the tasks,
the tags and their roles, and the driver. `{{.Name}}_test.yaml` holds the
acceptance tests — they run in **virtual time**, so an on-delay or a
loop's settling time is asserted exactly, in milliseconds.

{{end -}}
## Tests

`nautilus test` runs every `*_test.yaml` here. The fixture comes from
`nautilus.yaml` itself, so a retuned gain or a renamed tag can't drift
away from what the tests verify. Assertions are tag matchers
(`{near: 72.0, tol: 0.5}`) or ST expressions
(`ABS(TempC - TempSP) < 0.5`) compiled by the same compiler as your logic
— put reusable ones in a library file as a `FUNCTION` returning `BOOL`.

Open the folder in VS Code with the **nautilus IEC 61131-3** extension for
diagnostics, the graphical FBD/ladder editors, live tag values, and
online edits against the running controller.
{{if .Deploy}}
## Deploy

Every push to main that passes `check` + `test` becomes a controller
image on GHCR (`.github/workflows/deploy.yml`). `deploy/k8s.yaml` runs it
as a **redundant pair**: two replicas elect a scanning leader through a
Lease, setpoints and online edits ride a ConfigMap across restarts and
failovers, and the Service reaches live data on either replica.

```sh
kubectl apply -f deploy/k8s.yaml   # after pointing image: at your GHCR
```

Uncomment the rollout step in deploy.yml (and add a `KUBE_CONFIG`
secret) to close the loop: a merged PR is running on the plant floor
minutes later, by sha, with `kubectl rollout undo` as the way back.
{{end}}

When you need a custom field bus or richer simulation physics, that's the
Go SDK: `nautilus new --template sdk` (or `sdk-demo`) scaffolds it, and
the IEC logic here carries over unchanged.
